In article <ribtm-030893140638@scm41-2.phys.ttu.edu> ribtm@ttacs.ttu.edu (Greg Vaughn) writes:

>  Has anyone else noticed that with polymorph control you can name the title
>  of a character class?  The first time I tried it I chose to polymorph my
>  barbarian into a captain.  It had no effect but to raise my experience!

Hurm. I tried this in discovery mode and found that:

	1) All level titles, class names and names of "human" monsters
	   (such as soldiers, shopkeepers, nurses and the Wiz) are treated
	   the same way by poly control, to wit: you turn into a human
	   of the same class with stats and experience changed by a random
	   factor.
	2) There is no control over what level you end up, save for that
	   it seems to always be within 3 levels of where you were before.
	3) Changing classes via polymorph doesn't work.
	4) A human cannot polymorph into an elf, and vice-versa. Attempts
	   to do this (by, for example, asking to polymorph into "elf"
	   or "edhel") are met with:
		You cannot polymorph into that.--More--
	5) Attempts to polymorph into unrecognized things (including
	   monsters seen only while hallucinating) yields:
		I've never heard of such monsters.--More--

A quick perusal of the source seems to bear this out. 
